<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>Independent Accountants  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;On March&nbsp;10, 2003, the general partner engaged the accounting firm of McGladrey&nbsp;&amp; Pullen LLP as the Fund's independent accountants.
McGladrey&nbsp;&amp; Pullen replaces the Fund's previous accountants Taglia and Associates. The substitution of accounting firms was made in connection with the undertaking of this offering. In addition
to auditing the Fund's financial statements for the fiscal year ended December&nbsp;31, 2002, McGladrey&nbsp;&amp; Pullen also reaudited the Fund's financial statements for the fiscal years ended
December&nbsp;31, 2001 and 2000. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In
connection with the Fund's audits for the fiscal years ended December&nbsp;31, 2001 and 2000, there were no disagreements with Taglia and Associates on any matter of accounting
principles or practices, financial statement disclosure or auditing scope or procedure, which disagreements if not resolved to the satisfaction of Taglia and Associates would have caused them to make
reference to the disagreement in their report on the Fund's financial statements for those years. The reports of Taglia and Associates on the Fund's financial statements for the past two years
contained no adverse opinion or disclaimer of opinion and were not qualified or modified as to uncertainty, audit scope, or accounting principles. During the two most recent fiscal years, there have
been no reportable events, as defined in Regulation&nbsp;S-K Item 304(a)(1)(v). </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><A
NAME="dm2420_the_trading_advisors"> </A>
<A NAME="toc_dm2420_2"> </A>
<BR></FONT><FONT SIZE=2><B>THE TRADING ADVISORS    <BR>  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The general partner has retained Rabar Market Research,&nbsp;Inc., EMC Capital Management,&nbsp;Inc., Eckhardt Trading Company and Graham Capital Management,
L.P. as the Fund's trading advisors. Rabar has been trading on behalf of the Fund since January&nbsp;1989, EMC has been trading on behalf of the Fund since January&nbsp;1989 and ETC has been
trading on behalf of the Fund since April&nbsp;1998. It is expected that Graham will begin trading for the Fund immediately following the first closing date of this offering. As of
February&nbsp;28, 2003, the Fund's assets were allocated among the trading advisors as follows: Rabar 38%, EMC 26% and ETC 36%. It is anticipated that approximately the first $5&nbsp;million of
the initial proceeds of this offering will be allocated to Graham for trading, although the actual amount may be higher or lower. Initial offering proceeds above this amount are expected to be
allocated to the four trading advisors in roughly the above proportions, taking into account the initial amount allocated to Graham, or in such other proportions that the general partner determines. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The
advisory contracts authorize the general partner to reallocate assets among the trading advisors monthly as it determines in its sole discretion upon 10&nbsp;days' prior written
notice to the affected trading advisors. However, no reallocation of assets will be made to a trading advisor if the trading advisor determines that the amount of the reallocated assets, together with
other assets that are already under the trading advisor's management or which, pursuant to firm written commitments, will be placed under the trading advisor's discretion, would exceed the total
amount of funds the trading advisor could manage without detriment to the accounts it manages. For example, ETC recently informed the general partner that it may limit the amount of additional assets
that it will manage. The general partner therefore may be limited in its ability to allocate additional Fund assets to ETC. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Because
the advisory contracts also provide for reallocation upon termination of a trading advisor's advisory contract, it is possible that, during the terms of the advisory contracts,
the percentage of assets managed by the trading advisors may vary, perhaps substantially, from the current allocations. The advisory contracts are for a term of one year and are automatically
renewable for successive one-year terms until terminated. The advisory contracts generally provide that either party may terminate the advisory contract at any time upon no less than
60&nbsp;days' written notice. </FONT></P>

<P ALIGN="CENTER"><FONT SIZE=2>-23-</FONT></P>

