<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The
first month of 2000 proved to be a highly volatile environment as the uncertainty surrounding Year 2000 issues peaked, then waned. The Fund had a negative month to start the year
with losses in the Dax, the Hang Seng and the NASDAQ. Gains in traditional commodities such as soybean meal, corn and cotton helped to offset these losses. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The
volatility seen in January continued into February, providing opportunities in some markets but not in others. The Fund produced very subdued profits for the month, with most gains
coming from the energy markets as crude oil prices posted 9-year highs amidst supply concerns. Other profits were garnered in the Dax, Japanese yen and British pound. The largest losing
positions held by the Fund were in long-term interest rate instruments. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;March
started well but profits dissolved into losses by month end. Several sectors were strong early in the month, including energies, stock indices and currencies. Energies and stocks
turned around abruptly mid-month. Of all the positions that held promise early in the month, the Mexican peso proved to be the only sustainable trend. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;April
was marked by further losses in the Fund amid high levels of volatility. A legal ruling against Microsoft created losses in the NASDAQ, which had a ripple effect on equity indices
in the Far East. The Nikkei and the Hang Seng suffered their largest one-day decline since Black Monday in 1987. Some of the losses incurred by the Fund were offset by gains in the
Australian dollar, British pound and sugar. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In
May, the Fund posted positive returns led by a long natural gas position. Warmer than usual temperatures for early May in the United States and a steady decline in gas reserves sent
prices dramatically higher. Profits were also made via short Euribor and short British pound positions. The Fund incurred losses via a long nickel position, as a mining strike in Canada was averted,
sending the metal down 11%. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;June
was a negative month for the Fund. The bulk of the losses were incurred in the gold and silver markets as the U.S dollar fell in response to an unexpected 0.2% increase in the U.S.
unemployment rate. Profits were made on the long side of the Canadian dollar and Eurodollar trades as U.S. economic data indicated signs of weakening. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In
July, the Fund sustained losses in the energy sector after an announcement that Saudi Arabia would increase crude production in order to bring down prices. Additional losses occurred
in cotton and coffee on weather concerns. Some profits were made in sugar as prices soared to two-year highs on forecasts that the world glut would begin to decline in 2000 through 2001. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The
Fund saw significant gains in August due to a strong U.S. dollar and resurgence in energy prices. A strong U.S. economy led to dollar gains, while refinery troubles on both sides of
the Atlantic led to supply concerns for heating oil going into the fall months. Losses were incurred in the Australian dollar as it weakened versus the U.S. dollar after a surprise rate hike by the
Reserve Bank of Australia and a stronger than expected retail sales number. </FONT></P>

