<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The
Fund experienced losses in September as trades in global debt contracts and currency positions moved against it. Additionally, the announcement by U.S. Energy Secretary Bill
Richardson that the United States would release 30&nbsp;million barrels of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve over the next month caused crude prices to fall $2.00, bringing heating oil and
unleaded gas prices down also. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;October
resulted in modest profits for the Fund, stemming mostly from the currency markets. Weakness in the South African rand, Euro, and Canadian, New Zealand and Australian dollars all
contributed the most significant gains for the month. Losses were generated mainly from the sugar and natural gas markets as previous rallies reversed into profit-taking opportunities for some
investors. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;November
proved to be the most profitable month of the year to date. U.S., British and Japanese debt all rallied due to slowdowns in these countries' respective economies. These
slowdowns also led to weaker equity prices, which allowed the Fund's short positions in the NASDAQ and Dax to contribute to the month's profits. Losses were generated mainly in the currency markets as
the U.S. dollar fell amid the weak stock environment. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;December
built upon November's gains resulting in a strong closing of the year for the Fund. European and U.S. interest rate futures continued their rallies on expectations that both the
ECB and Federal Reserve would continue to cut rates. Poor economic conditions in Japan pushed the yen lower versus the dollar, which added to the Fund's profits. Some losses did occur, most notably in
the energy complex, as mounting evidence of an economic slowdown reduced the likelihood of strong demand from the industrial sector. Positive performance in December contributed to a profitable fourth
quarter and to a profitable year. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>Critical Accounting Policies  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Fund's critical accounting policies are set forth in Note&nbsp;1 to the financial statements beginning at page F-1 of this prospectus. The
Fund's financial statements are prepared in accordance with accounting principles generally accepted in the United States, which require the use of certain estimates and assumptions that affect the
reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements made by the Fund's management. The majority of the Fund's
positions will be exchange-traded futures contracts, which will be valued daily at settlement prices published by the exchanges. Any spot and forward foreign currency contracts held by the Fund will
also be valued at published daily settlement prices or at dealers' quotes. Thus, the general partner expects that under normal circumstances substantially all of the Fund's assets will be valued on a
daily basis using objective measures. Futures, options on futures, and forward contracts are recorded on the trade date and realized gains or losses are recognized when contracts are liquidated.
Unrealized gains
or losses on open contracts, which equals the difference between contract trade price and market price at the date of the statement of financial condition, are included in equity in broker trading
accounts. Any change in net unrealized gain or loss from the preceding period is reported in the statement of operations. U.S. government securities are valued at fair market value. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>Off-Balance Sheet Risk  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Off-balance sheet risk refers to an unrecorded potential liability that, even though it does not appear on the balance sheet, may result in future
obligation or loss. The Fund trades in futures and other commodity interest contracts and is therefore a party to financial instruments with elements of off-balance sheet market and credit
risk. In entering into these contracts the Fund faces the market risk that these contracts may be significantly influenced by market conditions, such as interest rate volatility, resulting in such
contracts being less valuable. If the markets should move against all of the commodity interest positions of the Fund at the same time, and if the Fund were unable to offset positions, the Fund could
lose all of its assets and the limited partners would realize a 100% loss. The Fund minimizes market risk through real-time monitoring of open positions, diversification of the portfolio
and maintenance of a margin-to-equity ratio that rarely exceeds 25%. All positions of the Fund are valued each day on a mark-to-market basis. </FONT></P>

