general, clearing organizations are backed by the corporate members of the clearing organization who are required to share any financial burden resulting from the non-performance by one of
their members and, as such, should significantly reduce this credit risk. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In
cases where the clearing organization is not backed by the clearing members, like some non-U.S. exchanges, it is normally backed by a consortium of banks or other
financial institutions. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In
the case of forward contracts, over-the-counter options contracts or swap contracts, which are traded on the interbank or other institutional market rather
than on exchanges, the counterparty is generally a single bank or other financial institution, rather than a central clearing organization backed by a group of financial institutions. As a result,
there likely will be greater counterparty credit risk in these transactions. The Fund trades only with those counterparties that it believes to be creditworthy. Nonetheless, the clearing member,
clearing organization or other counterparty to these transactions may not be able to meet its obligations to the Fund, in which case the Fund could suffer significant losses on these contracts. </FONT></P>

