the accuracy of factual representations made by the general partner. Katten Muchin Zavis Rosenman's opinion represents only its legal judgment and does not bind the Internal Revenue Service or the
courts. Certain tax consequences discussed below may vary in their application to each limited partner depending on such limited partner's particular circumstances, and the summary below therefore is
not intended to be a substitute for professional tax advice. You should consult with your own professional tax advisor concerning the U.S. federal, state and local tax consequences of investing in the
Fund. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>The Partnership Tax Status Of The Fund  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The general partner expects that the Fund will be classified as a partnership for U.S. federal income tax purposes and, therefore, that the Fund will not pay any
federal corporate income tax. It may be, however, that the Fund constitutes a so-called publicly traded partnership, in which case, the Fund generally would be subject to U.S. federal
income tax as a corporation, and distributions to limited
partners would be taxable as dividends, unless at least 90% of the Fund's annual gross income consists of "qualifying income" as defined in the Internal Revenue Code. The general partner anticipates,
but cannot assure you, that the Fund will satisfy the 90% test. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>Taxation Of Limited Partners On Profits And Losses Of The Fund  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Assuming that the Fund will be treated as a partnership for U.S. federal income tax purposes, each limited partner must pay tax on that limited partner's share of
any of the Fund's annual income and gains as determined for income tax purposes, even though the Fund does not intend to make current cash distributions. The income tax effects of the Fund's
transactions may differ from the economic consequences of those transactions. </FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2><B>Losses Allocated To Limited Partners  </B></FONT></P>

<P><FONT SIZE=2>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;You may deduct any of the Fund's tax losses only to the extent of your adjusted income tax basis in your interest in the Fund. Generally, your tax basis is the
total amount invested by you in the Fund, reduced (but not below zero) by your share of any Fund distributions, losses and expenses and increased by your share of the Fund's income and gains. However,
if you are an individual or other limited partner who is subject to at-risk limitations (generally, non-corporate taxpayers and closely-held corporations), you can
only deduct losses to the extent you are at-risk. Your at-risk amount is similar to tax basis, except that it does not include any amount borrowed on a nonrecourse basis by the
Fund or from someone with an interest in the Fund. </FONT></P>

